sequenceDiagram
  participant TA as Tab A
  participant TB as Tab B
  participant Gen as generatePhraseBatch
  participant Grade as gradeAttempts
  participant DB as Postgres pool max 4

  Note over TA,DB: FLOW A - batch generation, advisory lock
  TA->>Gen: generatePhraseBatch subject level pack
  TB->>Gen: generatePhraseBatch subject level pack
  Gen->>DB: unlocked nextSequenceBase read - prompt due-list only, staleness harmless
  Gen->>Gen: agent.generate prompt - LLM call, no txn, no lock held
  Gen->>DB: BEGIN tx Tab A
  Gen->>DB: SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock hashtext subject level pack cast bigint - A
  Gen->>DB: BEGIN tx Tab B - blocks acquiring the same advisory lock
  Note over DB: Tab A holds the advisory lock alone
  Gen->>DB: authoritative nextSequenceBase re-read via tx - A
  Gen->>DB: linkOrCreateTargetPhrases via tx - A - matches by lower trim text or inserts
  Gen->>DB: insertPhraseBatch via tx - A
  Gen->>DB: COMMIT A - advisory lock auto-released
  Note over DB: Tab B's BEGIN now proceeds, lock granted
  Gen->>DB: authoritative nextSequenceBase re-read via tx - B - now sees Tab A's committed rows
  Gen->>DB: linkOrCreateTargetPhrases via tx - B
  Gen->>DB: insertPhraseBatch via tx - B - non-overlapping sequence numbers
  Gen->>DB: COMMIT B
  Note over DB: DB backstops if the lock were ever bypassed
  Note over DB: unique index on phrases subject level pack sequence_number
  Note over DB: partial unique index on phrase_bank_entries subject level pack lower trim phrase_text where status is not mastered
  Note over DB: real FK phrases target_phrase_bank_entry_id to phrase_bank_entries id, ON DELETE SET NULL

  Note over TA,DB: FLOW B - grading, row-level FOR UPDATE lock
  TA->>Grade: gradeAttempts subject level answers
  TB->>Grade: gradeAttempts subject level answers
  Grade->>DB: insertAttempts A - outside any transaction, always applies
  Grade->>DB: insertAttempts B - outside any transaction, always applies
  Grade->>DB: BEGIN tx Tab A
  Grade->>DB: SELECT phrase_bank_entries WHERE id in entryIds ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE - A
  Grade->>DB: BEGIN tx Tab B
  Grade->>DB: FOR UPDATE select B - blocks on any row already locked by A
  Note over DB: rows always locked in id order, so two calls touching the same pair of entries can never deadlock against each other
  Grade->>Grade: applyAttemptToPhraseBankEntry pure deriver, packages core, unchanged - A
  Grade->>DB: updatePhraseBankEntryAfterAttempt via tx - A
  Grade->>DB: insertPhraseBankAppearance via tx - A
  Grade->>DB: COMMIT A
  Note over DB: Tab B's FOR UPDATE now proceeds, reads Tab A's committed state
  Grade->>Grade: applyAttemptToPhraseBankEntry pure deriver - B
  Grade->>DB: updatePhraseBankEntryAfterAttempt via tx - B
  Grade->>DB: insertPhraseBankAppearance via tx - B
  Grade->>DB: COMMIT B

  Note over DB: both flows share one pg Pool, max 4 connections
  Note over DB: every DB call inside either transaction body takes tx explicitly, never a default pool connection
  Note over DB: this is what keeps each transaction confined to the single connection holding its lock
  Note over DB: advisory locks in Flow A and row-level FOR UPDATE locks in Flow B are different Postgres lock namespaces
  Note over DB: they cannot deadlock against each other even if a generate and a grade run concurrently for the same scope
